1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,405
If your chest burns after meals and something sour keeps creeping up your throat, here's what's really behind it.

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:08,381
A ring of muscle called the lower esophageal sphincter keeps stomach acid where it belongs.

3
00:00:08,701 --> 00:00:13,703
When it relaxes too much, acid pushes back up into your esophagus - and certain foods make that muscle relax right on cue.

4
00:00:14,023 --> 00:00:18,683
Coffee is one of the most common triggers doctors hear about.

5
00:00:19,003 --> 00:00:23,649
Caffeine relaxes that sphincter muscle and ramps up acid production, especially when you drink it on an empty stomach.

6
00:00:23,969 --> 00:00:31,231
Try switching to decaf, or have your coffee after a meal instead of before - that alone takes a lot of the sting out.

7
00:00:31,551 --> 00:00:32,910
Spicy food is right behind it.

8
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:45,749
In one survey, nearly half of people with reflux said spicy meals made their symptoms worse - capsaicin irritates the esophagus lining as it goes down, and slows digestion, which gives stomach acid more time to work its way back up.

9
00:00:46,069 --> 00:00:49,852
You don't have to cut spice completely.

10
00:00:50,172 --> 00:00:56,710
Swapping hot sauce for milder herbs like basil or oregano still gives you flavor without the burn.

11
00:00:57,030 --> 00:00:59,595
Tomato sauce is the sneaky one - pizza, pasta, ketchup.

12
00:00:59,915 --> 00:01:05,298
Tomatoes are naturally acidic, and in patient surveys about one in ten reflux sufferers point to tomato dishes specifically as a trigger.

13
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:13,299
Look for a low-acid tomato sauce, or just stir a pinch of baking soda into your regular sauce - it helps neutralize some of that acidity.

14
00:01:13,619 --> 00:01:24,194
None of these need to disappear completely - just watch the timing and portions, and pay attention to what your body's actually telling you after each meal.
